
# Shell/python deployed to Linux must stay LF — a CRLF heredoc terminator
# makes bash report "unexpected end of file" only once it is on the server.
#
# ALL of them, not just scripts/. The pin was scripts/-only while the scripts
# that actually run on the box live elsewhere too — vaultchat-backend/deploy.sh
# and smoke-test.sh had no eol attribute at all, and the root apply*.sh none
# either. They survive today only because this checkout happens to have
# core.autocrlf=true, which is per-machine config, not a property of the repo —
# exactly the thing an attribute is for.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

# Protobuf schemas and the trees generated from them must stay LF, because
# `npm run proto:check` BYTE-compares fresh generator output against the
# working tree. Two independent line-ending faults made that comparison
# machine-dependent, and neither was the trailing whitespace it looked like
# (measured: 0 trailing-whitespace lines on either side, and `diff -w` empty):
#
#  1. protoc-gen-es writes LF, but core.autocrlf=true checks the committed LF
#     files out as CRLF. Every line then differs — 210 of 211 in cursor_pb.ts —
#     so the check fails on any Windows clone and passes on any Linux one.
#  2. Even with autocrlf off, the generator's output is MIXED: it copies
#     comment text verbatim out of the .proto sources, so a CRLF .proto puts
#     CRLF on exactly those lines (10 of 211 in cursor_pb.ts) and LF on the
#     rest. Pinning the gen tree alone would not have been enough; the schemas
#     have to be LF too.
#
# Pinning both ends makes the generator's bytes equal the working tree's bytes
# everywhere. The index already stores LF, so this renormalises nothing and
# commits no churn — `git status` stays clean across the change.
*.proto text eol=lf
lib/ccwire/gen/** text eol=lf
vaultchat-backend-go/internal/ccwire/gen/** text eol=lf
